{
  "id": 2,
  "slug": "into-the-middle",
  "title": "Into the Middle",
  "concept": "Why application servers sit in private subnets, and how security groups chain one tier to the next.",
  "guidance": "full",
  "region": "us-east-1",
  "azs": 2,
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "welcome",
      "text": "Altitude means distance from the internet. The EC2 instances sit in the mid band, in private subnets. They have only private addresses, so nothing on the internet can reach them directly: the only way in is through the load balancer. Let us prove it.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "first-run",
      "text": "Press \"Fly kites\". Click the red Cloudling first if you want to hear what it is after, then watch how far its kite gets.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "explain",
      "text": "The red kite could not aim at an EC2 instance at all: an instance in a private subnet has only a private address, and nothing on the internet can route to one. Getting in some other way would need something like a [bastion host](card:concept-bastion-host) and its credentials, and he has neither. So he tried the one public door, the load balancer, on SSH port 22. Its security group, alb-sg, allows only HTTPS 443, so it turned him away. But the green kites failed too: the load balancer has no targets yet.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "register",
      "text": "Click the load balancer and tick both EC2 instances as targets, one in each Availability Zone.",
      "highlight": "resource:alb",
      "pulse": ["targets:alb"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "targets-registered",
          "loadBalancerId": "alb",
          "atLeast": 2
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "chain",
      "text": "Now open either EC2 instance. Both wear the same security group, ec2-app-sg, so a rule you change there applies to both of them at once. It allows HTTP 80 from Anywhere: far wider than it needs. Remove that rule, then add HTTP 80 with the source set to the load balancer's security group, alb-sg. That means only something wearing alb-sg, the load balancer, may call the EC2 instances.",
      "highlight": ["resource:app-a", "resource:app-b"],
      "pulse": ["security-group:sg-app"],
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "security-group-allows",
          "securityGroupId": "sg-app",
          "ports": "http",
          "peer": {
            "kind": "security-group",
            "securityGroupId": "sg-alb"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "second-run",
      "text": "Fly the kites again. If an objective is still unticked afterwards, its hint will point at what is left.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "level-complete"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wrap",
      "text": "Each tier now trusts only the tier in front of it. The load balancer and the EC2 instances each have their own security group, and the instances' group names the load balancer's group as its source: that is called chaining, and it stays correct however the load balancer's addresses change. One group shared by different kinds of component would open each to traffic only the other needs, which breaks least privilege.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    }
  ],
